<section xml:id="sec-mod1-practice-set">
    <title>Practice Problem Set</title>
    <p>Estimated time: 100 minutes.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

        <p>
          This problem set is scaffolded from foundational to challenging.
          Problems are labeled by difficulty:
          <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Foundational (the essential mechanics of the
          module),
          <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Standard (complete proofs at the core level for
          this module), and
          <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Challenge (problems that combine the module's
          ideas or need one further idea of your own).
          All of the problems are part of the module. Work the
          <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> problems first: the <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> and
          <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> problems build on them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> For every proof problem, write
          your proof in complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled
          in the Worked Examples: announce your strategy, write in complete
          grammatical sentences, invoke each theorem by name, and justify
          every step. Label your proof strategy.
        </p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-ps-f">
        <title>Foundational Problems</title>
        <p>
          These problems practice the basic moves of the module: translating
          and negating quantified statements, naming a proof strategy, finding
          errors in a flawed proof, and writing short direct and induction
          proofs.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f1">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Translating Quantifiers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Translate each of the following into symbols, using the
            universe specified:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                (Universe: <m>\R</m>) <q>Every positive number has
                a positive square root.</q>
              </li>
              <li>
                (Universe: <m>\N</m>) <q>No natural number is
                both even and odd.</q>
              </li>
              <li>
                (Universe: <m>\R</m>) <q>For every two real numbers,
                their average lies between them.</q>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall x \in \R,\; x \gt 0 \implies \exists y \in \R,\;
                y \gt 0 \wedge y^2 = x</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\neg\bigl(\exists n \in \N,\; n \text{ is even} \wedge
                n \text{ is odd}\bigr)</m>, equivalently
                <m>\forall n \in \N,\; \neg(n \text{ is even} \wedge
                n \text{ is odd})</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\forall x \in \R\; \forall y \in \R,\;
                x \lt y \implies x \lt \tfrac{x+y}{2} \lt y</m>.
                An equivalent formulation without the hypothesis
                <m>x \lt y</m> is also correct, for example
                <m>\forall x \in \R\; \forall y \in \R,\;
                \min(x,y) \leq \tfrac{x+y}{2} \leq \max(x,y)</m>
                (the inequalities are non-strict because <m>x = y</m> is
                allowed).
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f2">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Quantifier Symbols to English</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Translate each symbolic statement into clear English prose.
            State whether each is true or false.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li><m>\forall x \in \R,\; x^2 \geq 0</m></li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists x \in \R</m> such that
                <m>x^2 = -1</m>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\forall x \in \R \;\; \exists y \in
                \R</m> such that <m>y^2 = x</m>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists y \in \R</m> such that
                <m>\forall x \in \R,\; y^2 = x</m>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <q>The square of every real number is nonnegative.</q>
                <em>True.</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <q>There exists a real number whose square is <m>-1</m>.</q>
                <em>False</em> (no real number has a negative square).
              </li>
              <li>
                <q>Every real number has a real square root.</q>
                <em>False</em> (negative reals have no real square root).
              </li>
              <li>
                <q>There is a single real number whose square equals
                every real number simultaneously.</q>
                <em>False</em> (no fixed <m>y</m> can satisfy <m>y^2 = x</m>
                for all <m>x</m> at once).
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f3">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Negation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write the negation of each statement as simply as possible.
            Then state whether the negation is true or false.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall n \in \N,\; n^2 + n \text{ is even}</m>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists x \in \R</m> such that
                <m>x^2 + 1 = 0</m>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists \delta \gt 0</m>
                such that <m>\forall x \in \R,\; |x - 2| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |x^2 - 4| \lt
                \varepsilon</m>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Negation: <m>\exists n \in \N</m> such that <m>n^2 + n</m>
                is odd. <em>False</em>: <m>n^2 + n = n(n+1)</m> is always
                even, since one of the consecutive integers <m>n</m> and
                <m>n+1</m> is even, and a multiple of an even number is even.
              </li>
              <li>
                Negation: <m>\forall x \in \R,\; x^2 + 1 \neq 0</m>.
                <em>True</em>: <m>x^2 \geq 0</m> for all real <m>x</m>,
                so <m>x^2 + 1 \geq 1 \gt 0</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Negation: <m>\exists \varepsilon \gt 0</m> such that
                <m>\forall \delta \gt 0,\; \exists x \in \R</m> with
                <m>|x-2| \lt \delta</m> and <m>|x^2-4| \geq \varepsilon</m>.
                <em>False</em>: the original statement is true (it expresses
                the continuity of <m>x^2</m> at <m>2</m>), so its negation is false.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f4">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Identify the Strategy</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each claim below, state which proof strategy (direct,
            contrapositive, contradiction) you would use and briefly
            justify your choice. Do not write the full proof.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                If <m>a + b</m> is even and <m>a</m> is even, then
                <m>b</m> is even.
              </li>
              <li>
                For all <m>x \in \R</m>, if <m>x^3 \gt 0</m>
                then <m>x \gt 0</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\sqrt{3}</m> is irrational.
              </li>
              <li>
                There is no largest prime number.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <alert>Direct.</alert> Write <m>a = 2j</m> and
                <m>a + b = 2k</m>; then <m>b = 2k - 2j = 2(k-j)</m>,
                which is even.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Contrapositive</alert> (or direct). Contrapositive:
                if <m>x \leq 0</m> then <m>x^3 \leq 0</m>. Monotonicity
                of cubing makes this clean.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Contradiction.</alert> Assume <m>\sqrt{3} = p/q</m>
                in lowest terms and derive <m>3 \mid p</m> and
                <m>3 \mid q</m>, contradicting <m>\gcd(p,q)=1</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Contradiction.</alert> Assume there is a largest
                prime <m>p</m>. Then there are finitely many primes
                <m>p_1, \ldots, p_k</m>, all at most <m>p</m>. Construct
                <m>N = p_1 \cdots p_k + 1</m>. Since <m>N \gt 1</m>, it
                has a prime factor, but no <m>p_i</m> divides <m>N</m>
                (each leaves remainder 1). So that prime factor is not on
                the list and exceeds <m>p</m>, a contradiction.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f5">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Direct Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove directly: if <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are both even
            integers, then <m>ab</m> is divisible by 4.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> Suppose <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are both
            even integers. Then there exist integers <m>j</m> and <m>k</m>
            such that <m>a = 2j</m> and <m>b = 2k</m>. Therefore
            <md>ab = (2j)(2k) = 4jk.</md>
            Since <m>jk</m> is an integer, <m>4 \mid ab</m>. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f6">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Spot the Error</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following is a <q>proof</q> of the Non-Theorem:
            <q><m>2^n \geq n!</m> for all <m>n \geq 1</m>.</q>
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Non-proof.</alert> Assume <m>2^n \geq n!</m>. Then
            <m>2^{n+1} = 2 \cdot 2^n \geq 2 \cdot n!</m>. Since
            <m>2 \geq n+1</m> for small <m>n</m>, we have <m>2^{n+1}
            \geq (n+1)!</m>. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            Identify all errors in this argument.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            There are three errors:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <alert>The theorem is false.</alert> For example,
                <m>2^4 = 16 \lt 24 = 4!</m>, so no valid proof can exist.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>The claim <m>2 \geq n+1</m> fails for <m>n \geq 2</m>.</alert>
                The argument says <q>for small <m>n</m></q> but does not
                specify which values, and the inductive step requires it
                for <em>all</em> <m>n</m>. This is the fatal step: passing
                from <m>n = 3</m> to <m>n = 4</m> would need
                <m>2 \geq 4</m>, and the Non-Theorem is false at <m>n = 4</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Missing base case and incomplete induction framework.</alert>
                The argument begins
                <q>Assume <m>2^n \geq n!</m></q> without establishing
                a base case first, and does not present the induction
                correctly. Here the base case is in fact true
                (<m>2^1 = 2 \geq 1 = 1!</m>), so its omission is a defect
                of the write-up, not the reason the Non-Theorem fails.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-f7">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Basic Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by induction: for all <m>n \geq 1</m>,
            <md>\sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2 = \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}.</md>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> We proceed by induction on <m>n</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Base case.</alert> For <m>n = 1</m>: the left side is
            <m>1^2 = 1</m>, and the right side is
            <m>\frac{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3}{6} = 1</m>. &#x2713;
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume
            <m>\sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2 = \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}</m>
            for some <m>n \geq 1</m>. Then:
            <md>
              <mrow>\sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k^2 \amp= \left(\sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2\right)
              + (n+1)^2</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} + (n+1)^2</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1) + 6(n+1)^2}{6}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{(n+1)\bigl[n(2n+1) + 6(n+1)\bigr]}{6}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{(n+1)(2n^2+7n+6)}{6}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \frac{(n+1)(n+2)(2n+3)}{6}.</mrow>
            </md>
            This is the formula with <m>n</m> replaced by <m>n+1</m>, so the
            inductive step is complete.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the principle of mathematical induction, the formula holds
            for all integers <m>n \geq 1</m>. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-ps-s">
        <title>Standard Problems</title>
        <p>
          These problems apply the same moves to longer arguments: quantifier
          order, convergence language, contrapositive, contradiction,
          biconditional, and induction proofs.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s1">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Quantifier Order</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each pair of statements, determine whether they have the
            same meaning. If they differ, describe the difference with a
            specific example.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A:</m> <m>\forall x \gt 0 \;\; \exists y \gt 0</m>
                  such that <m>y \lt x</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                  <m>B:</m> <m>\exists y \gt 0</m> such that <m>\forall x \gt 0,\;
                  y \lt x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A:</m> <m>\forall x \in \R \;\; \forall y \in
                  \R,\; x + y = y + x</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                  <m>B:</m> <m>\forall y \in \R \;\; \forall x \in
                  \R,\; x + y = y + x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <alert>Different.</alert> <m>A</m> is true (<m>y = x/2</m>
                works for each <m>x</m>). <m>B</m> is false: any fixed
                <m>y \gt 0</m> fails for <m>x = y/2 \lt y</m>.
                The difference is that in <m>A</m>, <m>y</m> may depend
                on <m>x</m>; in <m>B</m>, one <m>y</m> must work for all
                <m>x</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Same.</alert> Both say <q>addition of reals is
                commutative.</q> Swapping the order of two universal
                quantifiers over the same domain does not change the
                meaning.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s2">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Negating a Definition: Cauchy Sequences</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A sequence <m>(a_n)</m> of real numbers is a
            <term>Cauchy sequence</term> if
            <md>
              \forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists N \in \N
              \text{ s.t. } \forall m \in \N \;\; \forall n \in \N,\;\;
              (m \geq N \wedge n \geq N) \Rightarrow |a_m - a_n| \lt \varepsilon.
            </md>
            In words: from some point on, any two terms are within
            <m>\varepsilon</m> of each other. (Cauchy sequences appear in
            the definition of completeness at the end of the Bridge
            Reading, and again in <xref ref="ch-mod4"/>.)
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Write the negation (i.e., the definition of
                <q><m>(a_n)</m> is not a Cauchy sequence</q>), simplified
                so that no <m>\neg</m> symbol remains.
              </li>
              <li>
                Use your negation to prove that the sequence
                <m>a_n = (-1)^n</m> is not a Cauchy sequence. Say which
                <m>\varepsilon</m> you choose and, for a given <m>N</m>,
                which <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            For (a), negate one quantifier at a time from the left, and use
            <m>\neg(P \Rightarrow Q) \iff P \wedge \neg Q</m> on the
            implication. For (b), consecutive terms of
            <m>(-1)^n</m> are always 2 apart.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\exists \varepsilon \gt 0</m> such that
                <m>\forall N \in \N,\; \exists m \in \N \;\; \exists n \in \N</m>
                with <m>m \geq N</m>, <m>n \geq N</m>, and
                <m>|a_m - a_n| \geq \varepsilon</m>.
                Each quantifier flips, the condition <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>
                stays, and the implication becomes a conjunction.
              </li>
              <li>
                Let <m>\varepsilon = 1</m>, and let <m>N \in \N</m> be
                given. Take <m>m = N</m> and <m>n = N + 1</m>. Both are at
                least <m>N</m>, and one of them is even and the other odd,
                so one of <m>a_m, a_n</m> is <m>1</m> and the other is
                <m>-1</m>. Hence <m>|a_m - a_n| = 2 \geq 1 = \varepsilon</m>.
                Since <m>N</m> was arbitrary, the negation in (a) holds, and
                <m>(a_n)</m> is not a Cauchy sequence. (Any
                <m>\varepsilon</m> with <m>0 \lt \varepsilon \leq 2</m>
                works.)
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s3">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Contrapositive Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by contrapositive: for integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>,
            if <m>m + n</m> is odd, then exactly one of <m>m</m> or
            <m>n</m> is odd.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            The contrapositive is: if it is not the case that exactly
            one of <m>m, n</m> is odd (i.e., they are both odd or both
            even), then <m>m + n</m> is even. Handle both cases.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Strategy.</alert> We prove the contrapositive: if it is
            not the case that exactly one of <m>m, n</m> is odd, then
            <m>m + n</m> is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> Assume it is not the case that exactly
            one of <m>m, n</m> is odd. Then either both are odd or both
            are even.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>(i) Both even.</em> Write <m>m = 2j</m> and <m>n = 2k</m>.
            Then <m>m + n = 2(j+k)</m>, which is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>(ii) Both odd.</em> Write <m>m = 2j+1</m> and <m>n = 2k+1</m>.
            Then <m>m + n = 2j + 2k + 2 = 2(j+k+1)</m>, which is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            In both cases <m>m+n</m> is even. By contrapositive, if
            <m>m+n</m> is odd then exactly one of <m>m,n</m> is odd. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s4">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Contradiction Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by contradiction: <m>\sqrt{3}</m> is irrational.
          </p>
          <p>
            (The argument is similar to the proof that <m>\sqrt{2}</m>
            is irrational, but uses the fact that if <m>3 \mid n^2</m>
            then <m>3 \mid n</m>, where <m>\mid</m> denotes divisibility.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Strategy.</alert> Proof by contradiction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> Suppose, toward contradiction, that
            <m>\sqrt{3}</m> is rational. Then we may write
            <m>\sqrt{3} = p/q</m> where <m>p, q \in \Z</m>, <m>q \neq 0</m>,
            and <m>\gcd(p,q) = 1</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Squaring both sides gives <m>3 = p^2/q^2</m>, hence
            <m>p^2 = 3q^2</m>. Thus <m>3 \mid p^2</m>. Since 3 is prime
            and <m>3 \mid p^2</m>, we conclude <m>3 \mid p</m>. Write
            <m>p = 3k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>. Substituting:
            <m>(3k)^2 = 3q^2</m>, so <m>9k^2 = 3q^2</m>, giving
            <m>3k^2 = q^2</m>. Therefore <m>3 \mid q^2</m>, and again
            since 3 is prime, <m>3 \mid q</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            But then <m>3 \mid p</m> and <m>3 \mid q</m>, contradicting
            <m>\gcd(p,q) = 1</m>. Therefore <m>\sqrt{3}</m> is
            irrational. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s5">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Iff Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove: an integer <m>n</m> is odd if and only if <m>n^2</m>
            is odd.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Strategy.</alert> Prove both directions of the biconditional.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>(<m>\Rightarrow</m>)</alert> Suppose <m>n</m> is odd.
            Then <m>n = 2k+1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>. So
            <m>n^2 = (2k+1)^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2+2k) + 1</m>,
            which is odd.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>(<m>\Leftarrow</m>)</alert> We prove the contrapositive
            of this direction: if <m>n</m> is even, then <m>n^2</m> is even.
            Suppose <m>n</m> is even, so <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer
            <m>k</m>. Then
            <m>n^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2)</m>, which is even. By contrapositive,
            if <m>n^2</m> is odd then <m>n</m> is odd.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both directions hold, so <m>n</m> is odd <m>\iff</m>
            <m>n^2</m> is odd. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s6">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Induction with a Shifted Base</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by induction: for all <m>n \geq 5</m>,
            <m>n^2 \lt 2^n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            Start your base case at <m>n = 5</m>. In the inductive step,
            you will need to show that if <m>n^2 \lt 2^n</m>, then
            <m>(n+1)^2 \lt 2^{n+1}</m>. It helps to show
            <m>(n+1)^2 \leq 2n^2</m> for <m>n \geq 3</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> We proceed by induction on <m>n \geq 5</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Base case.</alert> <m>n = 5</m>:
            <m>5^2 = 25 \lt 32 = 2^5</m>. &#x2713;
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume <m>n^2 \lt 2^n</m> for
            some <m>n \geq 5</m>. Since <m>n \geq 3</m>,
            <m>(n+1)^2 = n^2 + 2n + 1 \leq n^2 + 2n + n = n^2 + 3n
            \leq n^2 + n^2 = 2n^2</m> (using <m>3n \leq n^2</m> for
            <m>n \geq 3</m>). Therefore
            <m>(n+1)^2 \leq 2n^2 \lt 2 \cdot 2^n = 2^{n+1}</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the principle of mathematical induction,
            <m>n^2 \lt 2^n</m> for all integers <m>n \geq 5</m>.
            <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-s7">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Divisibility by Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove by induction: for all <m>n \geq 1</m>,
            <m>6 \mid (n^3 - n)</m>. (Here <m>6 \mid m</m> means 6
            divides <m>m</m> evenly.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Proof.</alert> We proceed by induction on <m>n \geq 1</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Base case.</alert> <m>n = 1</m>: <m>1^3 - 1 = 0 = 6 \cdot 0</m>.
            &#x2713;
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume <m>6 \mid (n^3 - n)</m>.
            We compute:
            <md>
              <mrow>(n+1)^3 - (n+1)
              \amp= n^3 + 3n^2 + 3n + 1 - n - 1</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= (n^3 - n) + 3n^2 + 3n</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= (n^3 - n) + 3n(n+1).</mrow>
            </md>
            By the induction hypothesis, <m>6 \mid (n^3-n)</m>. Also,
            one of the consecutive integers <m>n</m> and <m>n+1</m> is even,
            so <m>n(n+1)</m> is even and <m>6 \mid 3n(n+1)</m>. Therefore
            <m>6 \mid (n+1)^3-(n+1)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the principle of mathematical induction,
            <m>6 \mid (n^3 - n)</m> for all integers <m>n \geq 1</m>.
            <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-ps-c">
        <title>Challenge Problems</title>
        <p>
          These problems extend the module's ideas: repairing ill-formed
          statements, existence and uniqueness, proof by cases, strong
          induction, and a first look at induction in analysis.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-c1">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Fixing Bad Statements</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Each of the following statements is either false, ill-formed,
            or ambiguous. Identify the problem and write a corrected
            version that is both well-formed and true.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <q>For all <m>\Q</m>, there is an <m>x</m> with
                <m>x^2 \lt \Q</m>.</q>
              </li>
              <li>
                <q>There exists <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> real numbers,
                <m>xy \neq yx</m>.</q>
              </li>
              <li>
                <q>If <m>f</m> is continuous, it's differentiable.</q>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-c2">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Existence and Uniqueness</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Prove that there exists a real number <m>x</m> such
                that <m>x^3 - x = 0</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Prove that this <m>x</m> is not unique (i.e., there is
                more than one such <m>x</m>).
              </li>
              <li>
                Prove that for every real number <m>a</m>, the equation
                <m>x^3 + x = a</m> has <em>at most one</em> real solution.
              </li>
              <li>
                Prove that the equation <m>x^3 + x = 2</m> has exactly one
                real solution.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            (Parts (a) and (b) are an existence claim and the failure of
            uniqueness; part (c) is a uniqueness claim with no existence
            claim; part (d) combines the two.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            For (a), an example is a complete proof of an existence claim.
            For (c), use the uniqueness strategy from
            <xref ref="ex-mod1-sg-h3-1"/>:
            suppose <m>x^3 + x = a</m> and <m>y^3 + y = a</m>, and show
            <m>x = y</m>. Subtract the two equations and factor out
            <m>x - y</m>; then show that the other factor is positive by
            completing the square.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>x = 0</m> works: <m>0^3 - 0 = 0</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>x = 1</m> also works (<m>1 - 1 = 0</m>), and
                <m>1 \neq 0</m>. In fact
                <m>x^3 - x = x(x-1)(x+1)</m>, and a product of real numbers
                is <m>0</m> exactly when at least one factor is <m>0</m>, so the
                solutions are exactly <m>-1</m>, <m>0</m>, and <m>1</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Suppose <m>x^3 + x = a</m> and <m>y^3 + y = a</m>.
                Subtracting,
                <md>0 = (x^3 - y^3) + (x - y) = (x - y)(x^2 + xy + y^2 + 1).</md>
                Completing the square,
                <m>x^2 + xy + y^2 = \bigl(x + \tfrac{y}{2}\bigr)^2 + \tfrac{3}{4}y^2 \geq 0</m>,
                so the second factor is at least <m>1</m>, and in
                particular not <m>0</m>. Hence <m>x - y = 0</m>, so
                <m>x = y</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Existence: <m>x = 1</m> gives <m>1 + 1 = 2</m>.
                Uniqueness: by (c) with <m>a = 2</m>, there is at most one
                solution. So <m>x = 1</m> is the only real solution.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-c3">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Proof by Cases</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove: for every integer <m>n</m>, <m>n(n+1)(n+2)</m> is
            divisible by 6. Organize the proof in three steps.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Prove that <m>n^2 + n = n(n+1)</m> is even. (Consider two
                cases: <m>n</m> is even, and <m>n</m> is odd.)
              </li>
              <li>
                Prove that <m>n(n+1)(n+2)</m> is divisible by 3. (Consider
                the three possible remainders when <m>n</m> is divided by 3.)
              </li>
              <li>
                Combine (a) and (b). Be careful: you must explain why an
                integer that is divisible by 2 and by 3 is divisible by 6.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            Since <m>n^3 - n = (n-1)n(n+1)</m>, this gives a second proof of
            <xref ref="ex-mod1-ps-s7"/>. Which proof tells you more about
            <em>why</em> the result is true?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                If <m>n = 2k</m>, then <m>n(n+1) = 2\bigl(k(n+1)\bigr)</m>.
                If <m>n = 2k + 1</m>, then <m>n + 1 = 2(k+1)</m> and
                <m>n(n+1) = 2\bigl(n(k+1)\bigr)</m>. In both cases
                <m>n(n+1)</m> is even.
              </li>
              <li>
                Write <m>n = 3q + r</m> with <m>r \in \{0, 1, 2\}</m>. If
                <m>r = 0</m>, then <m>3 \mid n</m>. If <m>r = 1</m>, then
                <m>n + 2 = 3(q+1)</m>. If <m>r = 2</m>, then
                <m>n + 1 = 3(q+1)</m>. In each case one factor of
                <m>n(n+1)(n+2)</m> is a multiple of 3, so the product is.
              </li>
              <li>
                Let <m>m = n(n+1)(n+2)</m>. By (a), <m>m</m> is even, since
                it is a multiple of the even number <m>n(n+1)</m>. By (b),
                <m>m = 3b</m> for some integer <m>b</m>. If <m>b</m> were
                odd, say <m>b = 2j + 1</m>, then
                <m>m = 6j + 3 = 2(3j + 1) + 1</m> would be odd, a
                contradiction. So <m>b = 2c</m> for some integer <m>c</m>,
                and <m>m = 6c</m>.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-c4">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Strong Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove using strong induction
            (<xref ref="thm-mod1-strong-induction"/>): every integer
            <m>n \geq 2</m> is either prime or can be written as a product
            of primes.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-ps-c5">
        <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Looking Ahead: Induction in Analysis</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>a_1 = 1</m> and <m>a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n}</m> for
            <m>n \geq 1</m>. Prove by induction that <m>a_n \lt 2</m>
            for all <m>n \geq 1</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            (This is a preview of <xref ref="ch-mod4"/>: induction is a key tool for
            establishing bounds on recursively defined sequences, which
            then helps prove convergence.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


  </section>
